From 4e807b14f57cbf573adecd0ae5b23a4d8b6f7dc4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 13:13:40 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 110/180] Lighten the GPU ferrofluid shader, and fix the Canvas
 one's star-shaped crown

GPU ferrofluid was too heavy for lower-end GPUs and still not visibly
reactive. Three changes cut its per-frame cost substantially:

- SPIKES 8 -> 5 and MAX_STEPS 40 -> 24, since scene cost is spikes x steps
- Normal estimation switched from six paired +/- samples to the tetrahedron
  technique's four corner samples, the same gradient for a third less cost
- Rendered at RENDER_SCALE (0.5) of the real pixel size via a custom
  Modifier.layout + placeWithLayer, then scaled back up - raymarch cost is
  per-pixel, so this alone cuts it to a quarter, and a soft background
  crown loses little to the resulting blur

Combined with a modest reactivity pass (faster orbit, bigger wobble, bass
and transient pushed harder into spike height) this should be dramatically
cheaper while actually moving more, not less.

Separately, the lightweight Canvas Ferrofluid's spikes were reading as a
sea-urchin/star outline instead of a crown: at rest (no bass at all) peaks
reached 3.25-4.35x the pool radius while valleys sat at ~0.9x - a peak-to-
valley ratio nothing on a real ferrofluid photo looks like. It's that
ratio, not how tall a spike gets in isolation, that reads as "ferrofluid"
versus "throwing star". Brought the resting multiplier down to 1.15-1.65x
so the crown looks like a crown when nothing is playing loud, while still
letting bass punch spikes out dramatically on a hit (the "snappy, not
organic" reactive behaviour is unchanged, just rebalanced around a
sane resting shape).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
