From a168f94231e073c8642461f4878349e4e736cca8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 21:42:17 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 155/180] Merge a restored backup into the live database
 instead of replacing it outright

Restore used to copy the backup's database file straight over the live one
- fine for a manual backup (always complete), but automatic backups can now
be deliberately partial (AutoBackupCategories: history/local playlists/
synced playlists/stats each independently excludable). A straight file swap
of one of those would have deleted everything it left out, which is the
opposite of what "restore my stats backup" should do.

Restore now ATTACHes the validated backup database to the live one and runs
INSERT OR REPLACE per table (table list read from the backup's own
sqlite_master, not hardcoded, so a future new table doesn't need this
updated). A row the backup has overwrites the live row sharing its primary
key; a row it doesn't have - because that category was excluded, or because
it's simply newer than the backup - is left alone rather than removed. Works
the same for an old full backup and a new partial one: a full backup's
"missing" rows are just whatever's been added to the library since, and
never getting deleted was already the point.
