From d01c1443ff74666fb0233c6b5e8ff46511453cca Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 22:23:51 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 157/180] Simplify backup: drop the merge-restore and selective
 categories

Both were real, standing risk: merge-restore ATTACHed the backup database
and copied every table with a positional "SELECT * ... INSERT OR REPLACE",
which silently assumes both databases have identical on-disk column order -
not guaranteed just because they're on the same schema version, since two
databases can reach the same schema through different migration histories
(Room's table-rebuild migrations reorder columns to match the current
entity, ALTER-TABLE-only ones don't). A column landing in the wrong
position is exactly how you get a NOT NULL constraint failure that looks
unrelated to anything actually wrong with the data - which is what started
turning up in testing. Selective categories added its own separate risk on
top: raw DELETE statements against a temp copy of the live database,
unverified without a working local compiler all session.

Restore is back to the original, proven behavior: copy the whole validated
backup file over the live one, unconditionally. Automatic backup now writes
the exact same complete backup the manual Backup button always has -
writeAutoBackup just calls writeBackup - so there's one backup format and
one restore path again, nothing selective to keep in sync between them.
Removed the four AutoBackupInclude*Key preferences and the "What to
include" section along with the category logic.
