From 4720eeeef53ebc2da3a2161c6e84534f8bb181e1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 19:40:08 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 172/180] Fix VISIONOS being silently dropped from the stream
 chain

The VISIONOS client was added and put first, but playback still died ~60s in
with a source error. The client was never actually getting used: VideoDetails
required title/author/channelId/lengthSeconds/viewCount/thumbnail to be
present, and the clients that answer with a trimmed videoDetails - VISIONOS
among them - failed to deserialize the whole player response. That threw
inside YouTube.player(), playerResult.getOrNull() came back null, and the
loop moved on without a word, handing the stream to WEB_REMIX - whose URL is
exactly the one googlevideo serves for about a minute and then refuses. The
retry cap raised to 5 just repeated that same fall-through five times.

Everything past videoId is now optional, matching yuuichi-s/OuterTune. All
metadata still comes from MAIN_CLIENT, so a sparse videoDetails from a
fallback client costs nothing (both consumers already null-safe).

Also, from the same reference commit:
- Send Accept and Accept-Language on the media request alongside the
  User-Agent/Referer/Origin already sent. googlevideo weighs whether the
  fetch looks like the client the URL was issued for.
- Add ANDROID_VR 1.65.10 and widen the chain to both remaining VR builds.
  YouTube treats each ANDROID_VR version differently, so a second gives the
  chain somewhere to go instead of losing the client when one is refused.

And two of my own:
- validateStatus rejected a client outright when the deep-range probe
  (PROBE_OFFSET, past the first chunk) came back non-2xx. A 416 there just
  means the track is shorter than the probe offset, and a deep range can be
  declined for reasons that don't stop sequential playback - either way the
  client was dropped and the stream handed to a worse one, which is the very
  failure the chain exists to avoid. Now falls back to a first-byte probe and
  only rejects if that fails too; the mid-track 403 recovery in
  onPlayerError remains the backstop.
- Log which client actually resolved the stream, at warn so it shows up in a
  release logcat. Which client won is the first thing worth knowing when a
  stream dies partway through, and nothing was reporting it.
